# Resource storage follow-up

- Characterize Chromium storage exhaustion and eviction with a real-browser probe
  before promising recovery behavior. Transaction rollback for an injected
  `QuotaExceededError` is covered; quota override did not establish real eviction.
- Cold offline application boot remains separate from the loaded-app guarantees in
  `features/project/.context/resource-lifecycle-architecture.md`. It requires an
  authenticated-shell bootstrap policy for cached account/project authority; do not
  infer authorization from locally retained resource metadata.
- Rejected `submitted` namespace requests are retried on every journal change and
  every 30 s. Offline with two tabs, about a dozen identical retries ran in 85 s.
  They carry one operation id, so they are harmless; back the retry off if the
  chatter shows up in a profile or the server log.

- A document the server never accepted can be placed optimistically, then refused
  again (filed while its create was in flight, or a repair the server refuses). The
  route has already moved to the destination path; once the row returns to Unfiled the
  route tab reads "This destination is unavailable." until it is closed or reloaded.
  `routeContinuityDocumentId` binds only server identities, so the route cannot follow a
  local one back. The document's own tab shows the failure and its text. Make the route
  follow a local resource handle through a rollback if this shows up beyond the repair
  path.

- A document or folder put inside a folder whose create was refused settles to repair with
  it, each with its own mark, and repairing the folder does not re-send them. In Unfiled a
  child document sits under its refused parent but a child folder lists beside it, because a
  refused folder's place is read from its typed name alone. If writers hit this, repair a
  refused folder together with what waited on it and nest children under it.
